/**
 * @defgroup optimal_pre_pump_efficiency_calculator Base Achievable Pump Efficiency
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief HI 20.3-2020 base achievable efficiency from pump type and BEP flow.
 * @details
 * What generally attainable efficiency corresponds to this rotodynamic pump type and flow?
 * When a measured manufacturer curve is unavailable, the HI reference provides a starting estimate
 * before viscosity and specific-speed corrections. It does not measure an installed pump's condition.
 *
 * @heading{Result and Interpretation}
 * The result is efficiency in percent. A value of 83.99 means 83.99%, not a fraction of 83.99.
 * The reference relates to generally attainable performance at best-efficiency flow, with maximum
 * impeller diameter and clear water at 20 degrees C, as described by HI Figure 20.3.4.2.1a.
 * The full duty calculation applies the separate viscosity and specific-speed adjustments.
 *
 * @heading{Select the Reference Curve}
 * Each type selects its own HI 2020 reference data. PCHIP passes through every supplied point,
 * preserving the shape between points without fitting a global equation. Flow is the numerical
 * value in US gpm: interpolation does not take its logarithm, even when an HI figure uses a log axis.
 * The submersible-sewage and API double-suction data are distinct from sewage and stock, respectively.
 *
 * | Curve | Pump type | Inclusive flow range (US gpm) |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | A | End suction slurry | 100–80000 |
 * | B | End suction sewage | 100–52000 |
 * | C | End suction submersible sewage | 100–100000 |
 * | D | End suction stock | 400–22000 |
 * | E | Multistage boiler feed | 100–4000 |
 * | F | End suction ANSI/API | 100–5000 |
 * | G | Large end suction | 101–100000 |
 * | H | API double suction | 300–1300 |
 * | J | Double suction | 200–100000 |
 * | V | Vertical turbine | 200–40000 |
 *
 * Axial flow uses the V base table by the retained engineering mapping. It still uses the
 * nonvertical specific-speed correction; see @ref optimal_specific_speed_correction_calculator.
 *
 * @formula{hi2020-base-efficiency;
 * E_{pre}=\operatorname{PCHIP}_{type}(Q)
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{E_{pre}; Base achievable pump efficiency before duty corrections; \percent}
 * @symrow{Q; Liquid flow in the US gallon convention; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{type; Selected HI curve classification; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example}
 * For an ANSI/API pump (F), Q=1625 US gpm lies between 1000 US gpm at 80.9% and
 * 2250 US gpm at 84.36%. Tangents include neighboring points, rather than only the two interval
 * endpoints. PCHIP gives approximately 83.184%. The unrounded result enters
 * @ref optimal_pump_efficiency_calculator, which converts percent to a fraction once.
 *
 * @heading{Reference and Limits}
 * Tables follow the engineering developer reference PCHIP_Methodology_Dev._Ref, revised 2026-09-23,
 * based on HI 20.3-2020. C/H were digitized by engineering from Figure 20.3.4.2.1a; the others were
 * supplied as reference data. These are versioned tables, not an automatic update from an external source.
 * The H endpoint at 1300 US gpm is used exactly as supplied and requires engineering release confirmation.
 *
 * Endpoint inputs return the tabulated value exactly. Nonfinite or out-of-range flow returns NaN
 * (no estimate). Specified-efficiency, positive-displacement and unknown styles have no base table
 * and return NaN. Do not extrapolate, clamp a duty to a table edge, or interpret NaN as zero efficiency.
 * A measured or supplied efficiency remains usable outside these estimation ranges.
 *
 * @see pchip_interpolation
 * @see optimal_pre_pump_efficiency::calculate
 */
